  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dmitry Yakubovskiy is a Russian lawyer and businessman who developed residential projects around Moscow before investing in industrial coatings. Born in Bolshevo, he studied law and held legal and government posts before entering private practice. His property interests included Gorki-8 and City Land Group, through which he assembled and developed land.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Institutional biography and contemporary business interviews establish the durable career sequence."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Dmitry Yakubovskiy is a Russian lawyer and businessman who developed residential projects around Moscow before investing in industrial coatings. Born in Bolshevo, he studied law and held legal and government posts before entering private practice. His property interests included Gorki-8 and City Land Group, through which he assembled and developed land.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "An introduction through his Swiss lawyer brought Dmitry Yakubovskiy into contact with two Belgian scientists seeking help with intellectual-property rights. He became involved in their legal affairs and helped establish Thermission to commercialise their corrosion-resistant coating technology. The scientists and his brother, Stav Jacobi, were fellow shareholders.\n\nThe process used zinc-based coatings to protect metal components. It offered Yakubovskiy a route from legal work into an industrial venture, with the inventors retaining an economic stake. Thermission subsequently agreed a joint venture with truck manufacturer Kamaz in 2014, bringing the technology into a partnership focused on vehicle components.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 87,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "His detailed interview supports the introduction and shared venture; contemporary exhibition reporting independently confirms the manufacturing agreement. No claim of personal invention or later commercial success."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 97,
    "html": "<p>An introduction through his Swiss lawyer brought Dmitry Yakubovskiy into contact with two Belgian scientists seeking help with intellectual-property rights. He became involved in their legal affairs and helped establish Thermission to commercialise their corrosion-resistant coating technology. The scientists and his brother, Stav Jacobi, were fellow shareholders.</p>\r\n<p>The process used zinc-based coatings to protect metal components. It offered Yakubovskiy a route from legal work into an industrial venture, with the inventors retaining an economic stake. Thermission subsequently agreed a joint venture with truck manufacturer Kamaz in 2014, bringing the technology into a partnership focused on vehicle components.</p>\r\n"
  },
